diff --git a/docs/revision/paper-v2/main.tex b/docs/revision/paper-v2/main.tex
index 03e1a06..4560500 100644
--- a/docs/revision/paper-v2/main.tex
+++ b/docs/revision/paper-v2/main.tex
@@ -237,6 +237,12 @@ The registered estimates and decisions below come from the report that the tagge
 \caption{Q4: per-market lower bounds against the registered thresholds.}\label{fig:q4}
 \end{figure}
 
+\paragraph{Identity replacement and the B3--R3 gap (exploratory follow-up).} D2+H3 is D2 plus one switch $x$ that lets the attacker leave after a failed audit and return under a new identity. At the headline market, the D2+H3 search leaves \site{rs_hl_b3} more gain against B3 than the D2 search, and \site{rs_hl_r3} more against R3, so the B3 minus R3 gap is \site{rs_hl_g1} under D2+H3 and \site{rs_hl_g0} under D2. To see whether this holds on the six fresh markets, we ran after the report the D2 cells that budget cut~\const{3} had removed (B3, R3, and C$^*$ at $\rho=\const{0.2}$, each with a doubling check), under a plan and expectations written before any fresh D2 result existed but after the headline contrast was seen. This is a descriptive follow-up, not an independent replication, and it changes no registered decision. Pooled over the six markets with equal weights, the B3 minus R3 gap is \site{rs_g1} (\const{95}\% paired seed-bootstrap interval [\site{rs_g1_lo}, \site{rs_g1_hi}]) under D2+H3 and \site{rs_g0} ([\site{rs_g0_lo}, \site{rs_g0_hi}]) under D2 (doubled searches: \site{rs_g1_d} and \site{rs_g0_d}). The share of the gap that disappears without replacement, $S = \const{1} - G(\mathrm{D2})/G(\mathrm{D2{+}H3})$, is \site{rs_s} (doubled searches: \site{rs_s_d}); a value above one means the gap reverses slightly under D2. Replacement, $I = g(\mathrm{D2{+}H3}) - g(\mathrm{D2})$, adds \site{rs_i_b3} to the attacker's gain against B3, \site{rs_i_r3} against R3, and \site{rs_i_c} against C$^*$. The expectations set in advance score as follows. $I(\mathrm{B3}) > I(\mathrm{R3})$ on every market: ``\site{rs_exp_b}'' (\site{rs_sign_b} markets with the frozen searches, \site{rs_sign_bd} with the doubled ones). $I(\mathrm{C}^*) > I(\mathrm{R3})$ on every market: ``\site{rs_exp_c}'' (\site{rs_sign_c} and \site{rs_sign_cd} markets; fresh3 gives \site{rs_f3_c} with the frozen searches). Pooled $S > \const{0.5}$: ``\site{rs_exp_s}''. Within the D2 family, at the fixed parameters and search budgets, most of the estimated B3 minus R3 gap under the prescribed searches disappears when the attacker cannot replace its identity. The two searched winners can differ in every knob, not only $x$. With every other knob held fixed, switching replacement off in the D2+H3 winner lowers the gain by \site{rs_fx_b3} against B3 and \site{rs_fx_r3} against R3, and switching it on in the D2 winner raises the gain by only \site{rs_rev_b3} and \site{rs_rev_r3}. No D2 winner replaces its identity. $S$ is a ratio of two searched gaps and carries their search error (Table~\ref{tab:ph-reset}).
+\begin{table}[t]\centering\small
+\adjustbox{max width=\linewidth}{\input{tables/posthoc_reset.tex}}
+\caption{Exploratory follow-up, after the report: replacement $I = g(\mathrm{D2{+}H3}) - g(\mathrm{D2})$ per rule, its contrasts, the B3 minus R3 gaps $G$ under each family, and the share $S$, pooled over the six fresh markets with equal weights, with paired seed-bootstrap percentile intervals; frozen and doubled searches. $S$ is shown only when the lower bound of the pooled D2+H3 gap exceeds the preset floor.}\label{tab:ph-reset}
+\end{table}
+
 \paragraph{Which parts of R3 matter (Q2).} Under separately searched D2+H3 attacks, pooled over the fresh markets, the main effects on the gain are \site{r_q2_p} for the priority phase P and \site{r_q2_g} for newest-first residual targeting G, both reductions, and \site{r_q2_s} for pacing S, a small increase (X7-SC: \site{r_q2_s_x}). The two targeting parts are substitutes: the PG factorial contrast, half the difference between G's conditional effects, is \site{r_q2_pg}; G changes the gain by \site{r_q2_g_p0} without the priority phase and by only \site{r_q2_g_p1} with it, and the priority phase changes it by only \site{r_q2_p_g1} once G is in place (averaged over both pacing settings). R3's own comparison, $F_{111}$ minus $F_{011}$ (exploratory, from the evaluation records), is \site{r_dp} pooled: exactly zero on four markets, where both rules leave the attacker the same gain, and nonzero on fresh1 (\site{r_dp_f1}) and fresh3. Against the fixed attacks, the priority phase's effect given newest-first targeting is larger (\site{r_q2_fixed_pg1} under D2+H3 and \site{r_q2_fixed_pg1_x} under X7-SC, averaged over pacing), though still small next to newest-first targeting's effect without the priority phase (above). In the pooled, separately searched comparisons, given newest-first targeting the priority phase adds little at these audit ratios and capacities; at a lower audit ratio or a larger attacker capacity, half of a round's audits may no longer cover one provider's jobs, and the substitution need not hold. In the pooled contrasts, either P or G alone accounts for most of the measured reduction; adding the other contributes little. The pattern also appears in the fixed-attack contrasts averaged over the eight selected attacks (P: \site{r_q2_fixed_p}). Our registered conjectures (P negligible, the targeting parts additive, S helpful) failed as scored (Table~\ref{tab:predictions}); the stricter eligibility reading of Table~\ref{tab:pred-eligibility} leaves them not assessed, with the estimates unchanged. X7-SC agrees (Table~\ref{tab:q2-x7sc}).
 
 \begin{table}[t]\centering\small
